<BaseLayout title="Pulsar Timing Array Research — Jonas El Gammal" description="PTA non-Gaussianity, whitening, and accelerated Bayesian inference with JAX.">
  <div class="shell"><header class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">Non-Gaussian statistics and fast PTA inference</span><h1>Pulsar Timing Arrays</h1><p>I work on both the statistical limits and the computational bottlenecks of nanohertz gravitational-wave inference.</p></header></div>
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Can PTAs detect gravitational-wave non-Gaussianity?</h2><p>Our latest work asks whether model-agnostic statistics can distinguish an astrophysical population from a cosmological gravitational-wave background. The answer is largely negative—but the reason is scientifically useful.</p><ul class="paper-list"><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.05157"><strong>Are PTA measurements sensitive to gravitational wave non-Gaussianities?</strong></a><span>Under review · arXiv:2605.05157v2</span><p>Chiara Cecchini, Jonas El Gammal, Gabriele Franciolini, and Mauro Pieroni.</p></li></ul>
    <h3>Whitening is essential</h3><p>The Hellings–Downs response correlates PTA data. Applying distributional tests before decorrelation creates false positives even under an isotropic Gaussian null.</p>
    <figure class="figure figure--portrait"><img src="/assets/img/pta_ng_whitening_v2.svg" alt="Whitening calibration tests for the PTA non-Gaussianity analysis"/><figcaption>Without whitening, nominal p-values are miscalibrated. Whitening restores the expected null behaviour. Vector figure from the latest arXiv version. <a href="/assets/img/pta_ng_whitening_v2.pdf">Open PDF</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    <h3>Why non-Gaussian structure disappears</h3><p>There are two distinct effects to separate. Estimating the overall scale from the observed realization removes much of the variance information that would otherwise distinguish source populations. The broad pulsar antenna patterns then mix contributions across the sky, pushing intrinsically different populations toward the same effective Gaussian distribution.</p><p>The result does not say that every astrophysical background is exactly Gaussian, or that source populations contain no recoverable information. It says that the model-agnostic one-point distributional tests considered here have little power once correlation and scale are handled consistently. More targeted statistics, informed by the structure of the alternative model, remain a different question.</p>
    <figure class="figure figure--portrait"><img src="/assets/img/pta_ng_scale_v2.svg" alt="Central-limit and scale-estimation results for PTA source populations"/><figcaption>Scale estimation and the broad PTA response wash out most model-agnostic non-Gaussian information. Vector figure from the latest arXiv version. <a href="/assets/img/pta_ng_scale_v2.pdf">Open PDF</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2>Fast PTA inference</h2><p>Separately, I develop <a href="/software/discoverysamplers/"><strong>discoverysamplers</strong></a>, a clean interface between the JAX-enabled Discovery PTA likelihood and inference backends including Eryn, nessai, JAX-NS, and GPry. The aim is to keep the physical model and prior specification fixed while making it straightforward to compare inference strategies—from ensemble MCMC and flow-based nested sampling to GPU-compatible nested sampling and Gaussian-process emulation.</p>
    <div class="sampler-flow" aria-label="discoverysamplers architecture"><strong>Discovery model</strong><span>shared priors + adapter</span><div><b>Eryn</b><b>nessai</b><b>JAX-NS</b><b>GPry</b></div></div>
  </article>
